   "date": "2021-04-18T10:51:51Z",
   "subject": "Constraining item difficulties",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">Hello Everybody,</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">I tried to\r\nrun the dichotomous Rasch model with ‘mirt’ and a commercial Rasch program to compare\r\nthe outputs. The item and person parameters were not close at all. The constraint\r\non my software for model identification is that the mean of difficulty parameters is\r\nset to zero. But I think in mirt the mean of person parameters is set to zero. I\r\nguess this is the reason for the discrepancy. </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">How can I\r\nset the mean of item difficulties to zero in ‘mirt’?</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">Does ‘d’\r\nrefer to item difficulty parameters?  </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">Thanks for\r\nyour help.</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">Afshin</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\"> </span></p><div><br></div>-- <br><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Afshin Khosravi, PhD TEFL, MA TEFL, </div></div></div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "9Lzys2YZBQAJ",
   "from": "Phil Chalmers",
   "author": "b330c2028fed415b",
   "date": "2021-05-14T18:21:46Z",
   "subject": "Re: Constraining item difficulties",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Sun, Apr 18, 2021 at 6:51 AM Afshin Khosravi &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">afshinkho...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">Hello Everybody,</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">I tried to\r\nrun the dichotomous Rasch model with ‘mirt’ and a commercial Rasch program to compare\r\nthe outputs. The item and person parameters were not close at all. The constraint\r\non my software for model identification is that the mean of difficulty parameters is\r\nset to zero. But I think in mirt the mean of person parameters is set to zero. I\r\nguess this is the reason for the discrepancy.</span></p></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>One reason for sure. The other is likely that mirt uses a slope intercept form of the logistic model, logit(theta + d), rather than the usual logit(theta - b) that you would see in the literature. Just multiply the d values by -1 to get the difficulty parameters, or use coef(mod, IRTpars = TRUE) to let the package transform the coefficients and SEs.</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\"> </span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">How can I\r\nset the mean of item difficulties to zero in ‘mirt’?</span></p></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>If you insist, see this <a href=\"https://philchalmers.github.io/mirt/html/Three-Rasch.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://philchalmers.github.io/mirt/html/Three-Rasch.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792471704346000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3lmK3-mXRo0ppuERK_FlpR\">wiki page</a>. But this is just an identification constraint, so it&#39;s generally not required and will only make a difference if you plan on adding Bayesian prior distributions as these are parameterization dependent.</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">Does ‘d’\r\nrefer to item difficulty parameters?  </span></p></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>It&#39;s an intercept parameter. HTH.</div><div><br></div><div>Phil</div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">Thanks for\r\nyour help.</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\">Afshin</span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin:0cm 0cm 8pt;line-height:107%;font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:12pt;line-height:107%\"> </span></p><div><br></div>-- <br><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Afshin Khosravi, PhD TEFL, MA TEFL, </div></div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></blockquote></div></div>"
